<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Developer ID direct-download channel.

	  This file exists precisely because it must differ from the Mac App Store one. A Developer ID
	  certificate cannot authorise App Store-only entitlements — App Groups, iCloud containers,
	  push — unless a provisioning profile grants them. Sign an App Store-shaped entitlements file
	  with a Developer ID certificate and the binary launches, fails its entitlement check, and is
	  killed by the kernel immediately. That failure looks like a crash, not a signing problem,
	  which is what makes it so expensive to diagnose.

	  The sandbox is kept on even though this channel does not require it: iBase needs no exceptions,
	  so there is no reason to ship a less-contained binary outside the store than inside it.

	  Hardened Runtime is a build setting, not an entitlement — the lane asserts
	  ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME before building, because Apple refuses to notarize without it and the
	  error returned is unhelpfully generic.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
